X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:02.936
In this lesson, you will learn how to construct

2
00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:05.638
a Knowledge Graph from an example data set

3
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.008
on API services and their endpoints.

4
00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.310
Specifically, you will learn about

5
00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:13.913
a declarative approach for Knowledge Graph construction.

6
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.016
Alright, let's go.

7
00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.451
In this notebook, you will construct

8
00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.687
your first knowledge graph and visualize it.

9
00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.891
Here are two plots that represent parts of the knowledge graphs

10
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.394
that you will create during this lesson.

11
00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.863
To construct our knowledge graph,

12
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:33.266
first, we will see what the input data looks like.

13
00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.268
Then, you will learn how to

14
00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.270
model this data in the knowledge graph.

15
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.773
That is, we introduce the knowledge graph schema.

16
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:43.076
Finally, you will learn about a declarative approach

17
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:47.781
to map the input data to the knowledge graph following the schema.

18
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.084
In this course, you will use OData APIs

19
00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.286
and their EDMX specifications.

20
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.424
However, the methodology is not limited to OData

21
00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:00.527
and can be applied to other API specifications as well.

22
00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:04.097
Let's jump in the notebook and build our first knowledge graph.

23
00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:08.868
In this notebook, we start by importing all necessary packages.

24
00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:12.372
We use pandas to read the CSV files,

25
00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:15.075
rdflib to construct the knowledge graph,

26
00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:19.345
networkx and matplotlib for visualizing the knowledge graph

27
00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.449
and some helper functions to transform an RDF graph

28
00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.851
into a networkx knowledge graph.

29
00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.154
Let's start by reading the input data from these CSV files

30
00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:32.959
and see what information we have about each concept about these APIs.

31
00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.363
First, we read the services from the CSV file.

32
00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.066
We define that it's a comma separated file.

33
00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.268
We can specify the data type for

34
00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.771
the version column, which should be a string.

35
00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.474
And finally, we also define how

36
00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:50.076
to handle null values in the data.

37
00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:52.145
So let's take a look at what

38
00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.080
the data looks like that we just read.

39
00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.784
We take a look at the first row of the CSV file.

40
00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:01.454
We see that we have a name of the API service,

41
00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.457
we have a version, and we have a description.

42
00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.460
Here we have the PURCHASEORDER API

43
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.462
in version 4.0,

44
00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:13.066
and it says that it's an OData service for Purchase Order.

45
00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:16.002
Let's continue with the other input files.

46
00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:19.205
Next, let's take a look at the entity types.

47
00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.708
We again read from a CSV file.

48
00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:25.512
It's a comma separated file and we handle null values.

49
00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:28.615
For the entity types, there is a name

50
00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.518
and a service that this entity type belongs to.

51
00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.221
In this case, we have the purchase requisition item type,

52
00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.224
which belongs to the PURCHASEREQUISITION API.

53
00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.127
Next, let's take a look at the properties

54
00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:45.131
that provide more information about these entity types.

55
00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.233
Again, we read the CSV file,

56
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.336
comma separated file, and we handle null values.

57
00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.805
for the properties, we have the service

58
00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:56.109
that the property belongs to.

59
00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:59.112
We have the entityType to which this property belongs,

60
00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.114
a name of the property,

61
00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.383
in this case the cash discount.

62
00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.885
We have a label which is usually

63
00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:09.088
more descriptive, which is the cash discount percentage,

64
00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.090
We have the type of the property,

65
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.694
which is a Decimal type. We have the maximum length,

66
00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.096
whether it's a key of the entityType,

67
00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:20.700
and whether it's a selectable property by UI applications.

68
00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:25.738
Next, let's load the navigations. We again read the CSV file,

69
00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.840
and in this case we want to take a

70
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.642
look at the first two rows of the file

71
00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.946
in order to understand the types of navigations that there are.

72
00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:36.416
Let's take a look at the first two rows of the navigations.

73
00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:40.086
Navigations are defined for a service, they have a name,

74
00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.289
the entity type they navigate from,

75
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.892
and the entity type they navigate to.

76
00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.894
Finally, they have a multiplicity.

77
00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.297
And in this example, you can see that when we navigate from

78
00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:54.400
purchase order item to a purchase order,

79
00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.903
this multiplicity is one.

80
00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.506
This indicates that a purchase order item belongs

81
00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.176
to exactly one purchase order.

82
00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:05.778
While in the second navigation here, we see that

83
00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.880
we navigate from a purchase order

84
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.782
to a purchase order item,

85
00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:12.785
which is a one-to-many relationship,

86
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:15.588
indicated by the star in the multiplicity.

87
00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.790
That means a purchase order

88
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.893
can hold multiple purchase order items.

89
00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.797
Finally, let's take a look at the entity sets and load them.

90
00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.499
Again, we load the CSV file

91
00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:27.800
and we take a look at

92
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.302
the first row of the entity sets.

93
00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.904
Entity sets are logical containers

94
00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:35.308
for entities of a certain entity type.

95
00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.711
Each entity type belongs to exactly one entity set.

96
00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:42.215
Here we have the name of the entity set,

97
00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.217
the service they belong to,

98
00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:46.719
and the entityType that it belongs to.

99
00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.322
This concludes all input files.

100
00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.624
So now let's take a brief look at the statistics

101
00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.926
of the data that we just imported.

102
00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.929
Here, we set up a simple dictionary

103
00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:59.932
that maps the types that we just loaded

104
00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:04.932
to the number of rows within each of the corresponding data frames

105
00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:07.140
that were generated by loading these files.

106
00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.544
And we then show how many concepts we have per type.

107
00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.814
Here you can see, we have 39 services

108
00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.318
with a total of 101 entity sets

109
00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.620
and corresponding entity types.

110
00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.822
There are 126 navigations

111
00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.625
and more than 2,000 property types

112
00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.628
defined for these 101 entity types.

113
00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:31.764
Now, let's take a look how we can represent these concepts

114
00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:34.867
and their relationships in our knowledge graph schema.

115
00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.970
After getting an understanding of the input data,

116
00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.006
the next step is to define a

117
00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:42.075
schema or ontology for our knowledge graph.

118
00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:46.679
A knowledge graph schema defines the structure and relationships within this graph.

119
00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:49.349
It specifies the types of entities, nodes,

120
00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.651
and the types of relationships, the edges,

121
00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:53.753
that can exist in the graph.

122
00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:55.955
The schema acts as a blueprint,

123
00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:58.224
providing a formal representation of the

124
00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.860
domain knowledge being modeled and it enables

125
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.198
efficient querying, reasoning, and inference over the knowledge graph.

126
00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:09.235
OData APIs are described using the Entity Data Model,

127
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.971
from which we derive our knowledge graph schema.

128
00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:14.006
So let's take a look at the schema.

129
00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.776
The main entry point are services.

130
00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:21.481
The service advertise its concrete data model in a machine readable form,

131
00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:26.152
allowing generic clients to interact with the service in a well-defined way.

132
00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:29.389
For example, a service to retrieve

133
00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:33.292
and modify purchase order. Within a service, there can be multiple

134
00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:37.296
entity types. Entity types are the fundamental building block

135
00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:40.299
for describing the structure of the data

136
00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:43.136
provided by endpoints of the API service.

137
00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:46.539
The purchase order API service may have an EntityType

138
00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.641
for the purchase order header,

139
00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:51.844
which defines general information about the purchase orders,

140
00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.847
as well as an EntityType for the purchase order items

141
00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.849
that belong to the purchase order.

142
00:06:57.216 --> 00:07:01.587
properties define the shape and characteristics of data

143
00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:03.689
that an EntityType instance will contain.

144
00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.192
Among others, properties have a data type,

145
00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:08.694
a maximum length, and indicate whether they are key

146
00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:11.998
of the EntityType, as we just saw in the notebook.

147
00:07:12.298 --> 00:07:15.701
For example, a purchase order header may have properties

148
00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:19.605
for the creator, the creation date, and the currency of the order.

149
00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.809
A Navigation or navigation property

150
00:07:22.809 --> 00:07:25.144
is an optional property on an EntityType

151
00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:27.447
that allows for navigating from one end

152
00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:29.982
of an association to the other end.

153
00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:33.486
This allows, for example, to link the purchase order header

154
00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.188
to the purchase order items

155
00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:38.191
where an item belongs to exactly one header

156
00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:40.993
and the header may contain multiple items.

157
00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:45.531
Each EntityType is linked to exactly one EntitySet.

158
00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:48.234
The EntitySet are logical containers

159
00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.336
for instances of an EntityType.

160
00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:53.139
As a next step, you need to define mappings

161
00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.241
that will transform the input data

162
00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:57.944
to a knowledge graph using this schema.

163
00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:00.646
In this course, you will use SPARQL,

164
00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.950
the standard query language for RDF to define these mappings.

165
00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:07.954
If you're not familiar with SPARQL, let's have a quick introduction.

166
00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.857
SPARQL is the standard query language for RDF.

167
00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.559
You can think about it as the SQL for RDF,

168
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.762
and you may recognize some syntactical similarities.

169
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:19.565
Let's take a look at an example SPARQL query.

170
00:08:19.966 --> 00:08:22.335
The main part of the query is the WHERE clause,

171
00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:23.870
which defines the graph pattern that

172
00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.638
should be matched in the RDF graph.

173
00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:29.342
The core component of such graph patterns are triple patterns.

174
00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:32.345
Triple patterns are like RDF triples,

175
00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:35.348
but also allow for using variables,

176
00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.316
which should be matched in the graph.

177
00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:40.586
Variables are indicated with the leading question mark.

178
00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:44.190
In this example query, we want to match all nodes

179
00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.225
that are services and their names.

180
00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:50.229
In addition, we want to match the entity types of the services

181
00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.131
and their names as well.

182
00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:56.135
We can also visualize this graph pattern as a graph template.

183
00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:59.105
The query aims to match this template

184
00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:02.141
against the graph and retrieve all solution mappings.

185
00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:06.245
That is, mappings from variables to RDF terms,

186
00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:09.649
such that by replacing the variables with the terms,

187
00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:14.153
the templates yields a subgraph of the RDF graph.

188
00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:17.790
Moreover, SPARQL supports various query forms.

189
00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.760
This example uses a select query form

190
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.162
to just retrieve the solution mappings.

191
00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:26.465
Similar to SQL, the results of a select query

192
00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:28.467
can be represented as a table

193
00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:31.671
where the columns correspond to the variables in the query.

194
00:09:31.938 --> 00:09:33.739
and the rows to the solutions.

195
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.742
They are also INSERT queries for

196
00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:39.445
updating the graph by inserting additional triples.

197
00:09:39.946 --> 00:09:42.848
In this lesson, you will use the CONSTRUCT query form

198
00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:45.751
to construct the graph. Let's take a look at it.

199
00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:50.222
In a CONSTRUCT SPARQL query, you can use the triple patterns

200
00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:51.924
to define the shape of the graph

201
00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:53.593
to be constructed in the CONSTRUCT clause.

202
00:09:54.193 --> 00:09:57.196
In this example, we create a service entity

203
00:09:57.296 --> 00:10:01.200
and add a description, a version, and a name to it.

204
00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:03.769
Moreover, we can use a BIND statement

205
00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:05.805
in the WHERE clause of the query

206
00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:08.407
to create a unique identifier, the URI,

207
00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:11.811
of the entity to be added to the graph.

208
00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:14.447
Shown below the query text is the

209
00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:16.849
graph pattern that is defined in the query.

210
00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:18.751
Let's see now how we map

211
00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:20.753
the input data to this graph pattern.

212
00:10:21.253 --> 00:10:24.624
In our mapping function, the variables in the graph pattern

213
00:10:24.624 --> 00:10:29.028
are replaced by the values of the columns from the CSV file.

214
00:10:29.562 --> 00:10:32.331
That is, per row in the CSV file,

215
00:10:32.531 --> 00:10:36.135
we get one instantiation which is added to our knowledge graph.

216
00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:38.838
Here, this is shown for a single row

217
00:10:38.838 --> 00:10:41.741
which defines the purchase order API service

218
00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:44.043
that we just saw in the notebook

219
00:10:44.043 --> 00:10:47.046
with the name, the version, and a description.

220
00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:50.950
Now, let's go back to the notebook and do this in practice.

221
00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.352
We can now set up the first construct query

222
00:10:53.452 --> 00:10:55.154
that we just saw to generate

223
00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:57.156
the RDF triples for the services data.

224
00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.892
This corresponds to the query that you just saw.

225
00:11:00.259 --> 00:11:03.062
We have the service, we added a description to the service,

226
00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:05.164
a version and the name.

227
00:11:05.731 --> 00:11:08.401
Now, let's take a look at the transform function

228
00:11:08.401 --> 00:11:12.004
which takes the input data frame and the query as input

229
00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:14.473
and generates RDF triples for these inputs.

230
00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.842
This transform function takes the data frame

231
00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:20.046
as input as well as the construct_query.

232
00:11:20.646 --> 00:11:22.848
In addition, we can also specify whether we want

233
00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.252
to just construct the graph for the first row,

234
00:11:26.485 --> 00:11:30.289
of the input data. First, we set up a query graph

235
00:11:30.389 --> 00:11:32.558
and the result graph, and the

236
00:11:32.558 --> 00:11:35.094
result graph will hold the constructed data.

237
00:11:35.494 --> 00:11:37.296
We first parse the query.

238
00:11:37.496 --> 00:11:40.900
We then get all the headers from the data frame,

239
00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.970
and then we iterate over each row in the data frame.

240
00:11:45.337 --> 00:11:48.841
First, for the row, we construct a mapping

241
00:11:49.141 --> 00:11:52.278
from the variable or the column name

242
00:11:52.278 --> 00:11:54.346
to the value in this row.

243
00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:59.185
We then replace the variables by the values in the query

244
00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:04.123
using the initBindings provided by rdflib.

245
00:12:04.223 --> 00:12:08.327
We execute the query and for each result of the query,

246
00:12:08.427 --> 00:12:11.831
we add the corresponding triple to the result graph.

247
00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:16.235
Once finished, we return the complete result_graph

248
00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:20.172
after we processed all rows in the input DataFrame.

249
00:12:20.573 --> 00:12:23.375
Let's execute the function for just the first row

250
00:12:23.375 --> 00:12:26.278
of the services DataFrame and print the results.

251
00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:30.349
You see the resulting triples that are created

252
00:12:30.349 --> 00:12:32.351
using the turtle serialization.

253
00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:35.321
We created a service for purchase order.

254
00:12:35.621 --> 00:12:37.923
We added the triple of the description,

255
00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:39.525
a triple for the name,

256
00:12:39.925 --> 00:12:41.827
as well as a triple for the version.

257
00:12:42.128 --> 00:12:44.630
Next, we process the entire data frame

258
00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:47.600
and add the constructed triples to our knowledge graph

259
00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:50.002
kg, which we just defined before.

260
00:12:50.903 --> 00:12:52.605
Here, we call the transform function

261
00:12:52.905 --> 00:12:54.907
for the services data frame

262
00:12:55.107 --> 00:12:57.409
and the corresponding construct query.

263
00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:01.313
And we add the triples constructed to the knowledge graph.

264
00:13:01.614 --> 00:13:04.016
Next, we also print the number of triples

265
00:13:04.116 --> 00:13:06.318
that we just added to the knowledge graph.

266
00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:11.123
And by processing the entire data frame for the services,

267
00:13:11.123 --> 00:13:14.426
we created a knowledge graph of 156 triples.

268
00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:18.030
Let's continue the process with the remaining data frames.

269
00:13:18.497 --> 00:13:22.101
Here, we specify the construct query for the entity sets.

270
00:13:22.401 --> 00:13:23.969
We again call the transform function

271
00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.472
and print the size of the Knowledge Graph

272
00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:29.275
after adding the entity sets to the Knowledge Graph.

273
00:13:29.675 --> 00:13:32.278
As you can see, the size of the Knowledge Graph increases.

274
00:13:32.578 --> 00:13:34.280
We now have more than 400 triples.

275
00:13:34.947 --> 00:13:38.851
Similarly, we will process the entity types.

276
00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:43.189
Again, we specify the construct query, we run the transform function

277
00:13:43.489 --> 00:13:46.158
and add it to the knowledge graph that we already have

278
00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:49.895
and print its size. we end up with a bigger knowledge graph.

279
00:13:49.895 --> 00:13:52.264
We already have more than 700 triples.

280
00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:54.667
Now, we do the same thing for the properties

281
00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:56.368
and let's see what the results are.

282
00:13:56.769 --> 00:13:59.872
Now, we have more than 14,000 triples in the graph.

283
00:13:59.972 --> 00:14:02.875
This is because we had more than 2,000 properties

284
00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:06.679
and they add a substantial number of triples to the graph.

285
00:14:06.946 --> 00:14:09.448
We repeat the same thing for the navigations

286
00:14:09.748 --> 00:14:12.718
and end up with even more triples in the graph.

287
00:14:13.219 --> 00:14:17.122
Now, you've successfully constructed the knowledge graph of business APIs.

288
00:14:17.623 --> 00:14:21.727
Our AI agent could already leverage it to interact with individual APIs.

289
00:14:22.127 --> 00:14:25.431
However, it still lacks the context of how these APIs are

290
00:14:25.731 --> 00:14:27.533
used in business processes.

291
00:14:27.833 --> 00:14:30.536
Consider the entity sets for purchase requisition

292
00:14:30.636 --> 00:14:32.538
and purchase order, which have

293
00:14:32.738 --> 00:14:35.241
a dependency relationship in the process

294
00:14:35.541 --> 00:14:37.743
of procurement of direct materials.

295
00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:41.647
Let's visualize this disconnectedness by plotting the subgraphs

296
00:14:41.947 --> 00:14:44.149
induced by these two entity sets.

297
00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:46.919
First, we use a SPARQL query to

298
00:14:46.919 --> 00:14:49.388
retrieve the entity set nodes from the graph

299
00:14:49.588 --> 00:14:52.258
for purchase order and purchase requisition.

300
00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:55.427
Here, we define a SPARQL query template

301
00:14:56.061 --> 00:14:58.364
where we can filter by the name.

302
00:14:58.631 --> 00:15:02.935
We first run this and filter for the purchase order entity set

303
00:15:02.935 --> 00:15:06.939
to get the PO_node, the purchase order node.

304
00:15:06.939 --> 00:15:10.943
And then we also run this for the purchase requisition entity set,

305
00:15:10.943 --> 00:15:14.847
which we will store as the PR_node, purchase requisition node.

306
00:15:15.281 --> 00:15:17.783
Let's run this and we see the URI,

307
00:15:17.783 --> 00:15:21.053
the unique identifier for the purchase order entity set

308
00:15:21.053 --> 00:15:25.057
and the URI for the purchase requisition entity set.

309
00:15:25.557 --> 00:15:27.559
Next, we construct the subgraph

310
00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:30.062
from the knowledge graph induced by these two nodes.

311
00:15:30.462 --> 00:15:34.266
Here we first transform the RDF graph into a NetworkX graph

312
00:15:34.266 --> 00:15:36.669
in order to visualize it.

313
00:15:36.669 --> 00:15:41.240
We then construct the subgraph induced by the purchase order node,

314
00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.642
which we just retrieved from the graph.

315
00:15:43.642 --> 00:15:47.947
We then also construct the subgraph for the purchase requisition node,

316
00:15:47.947 --> 00:15:50.449
and then we just combine them into a single graph

317
00:15:50.449 --> 00:15:52.551
for the purpose of visualization.

318
00:15:52.885 --> 00:15:55.688
To visualize the graphs, we use the netgraph library,

319
00:15:56.388 --> 00:15:58.057
which we'll import here.

320
00:15:59.058 --> 00:16:01.460
We now set up the visualization.

321
00:16:02.061 --> 00:16:03.963
We define the colors of the nodes.

322
00:16:04.263 --> 00:16:05.831
We use different node shapes to

323
00:16:05.831 --> 00:16:07.666
better distinguish the nodes in the graph,

324
00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:10.369
and we also use different sizes

325
00:16:10.369 --> 00:16:12.771
just for the ease of understanding the visualization.

326
00:16:13.272 --> 00:16:16.075
And finally, we set up the graph using netgraph

327
00:16:16.375 --> 00:16:18.077
and show the resulting plot.

328
00:16:18.377 --> 00:16:20.679
In this visualization, you see blue nodes

329
00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.983
that correspond to the purchase requisition API

330
00:16:24.183 --> 00:16:27.786
and gray nodes that correspond to the purchase order API.

331
00:16:28.187 --> 00:16:30.589
You also see different shapes of nodes.

332
00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:33.592
For example, this circle node here in the center

333
00:16:34.193 --> 00:16:37.196
corresponds to the actual API service.

334
00:16:37.296 --> 00:16:39.898
It is connected to the different entity

335
00:16:40.265 --> 00:16:43.068
types indicated as square nodes,

336
00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:45.771
and for each of those entity types,

337
00:16:45.771 --> 00:16:48.273
you see a variety of properties

338
00:16:48.273 --> 00:16:51.043
that are indicated with this upward triangle.

339
00:16:51.043 --> 00:16:55.247
In addition, you see this larger downward triangle

340
00:16:55.247 --> 00:16:57.249
that connects different entity types,

341
00:16:57.249 --> 00:16:59.752
and this corresponds to the navigations

342
00:16:59.752 --> 00:17:01.754
between the different entity types.

343
00:17:02.154 --> 00:17:05.357
As you can see, the purchase requisition APIs

344
00:17:05.657 --> 00:17:08.961
are not connected to the purchase order APIs

345
00:17:09.361 --> 00:17:10.963
in the knowledge graph.

346
00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:14.366
I encourage you to try out searching for other entity sets

347
00:17:14.366 --> 00:17:16.668
in the knowledge graph and plotting them to see

348
00:17:16.668 --> 00:17:19.438
how they are structurally represented in the knowledge graph.

349
00:17:19.505 --> 00:17:22.408
Let's zoom out and take a look at the entire knowledge graph

350
00:17:22.408 --> 00:17:25.411
that you will end up by the end of the next lesson,

351
00:17:25.411 --> 00:17:27.379
which also includes this connectedness

352
00:17:27.679 --> 00:17:29.581
using the business processes.

353
00:17:30.215 --> 00:17:32.284
So let's take a look at a sample

354
00:17:32.284 --> 00:17:34.319
of a thousand edges of this knowledge graph.

355
00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:38.323
Now you can see the sample of the entire knowledge graph

356
00:17:38.924 --> 00:17:40.926
which has a very connected core,

357
00:17:41.326 --> 00:17:44.530
which corresponds to the connections of the business processes

358
00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:46.632
that you will add in the next lesson.

359
00:17:47.032 --> 00:17:50.235
As of now, the knowledge graph is an rdflib graph,

360
00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:54.039
which is held in memory. For sharing and reusing the knowledge graph,

361
00:17:54.039 --> 00:17:58.143
you can materialize it on disk using any RDF serialization.

362
00:17:58.477 --> 00:18:01.013
Here, we serialize the knowledge graph

363
00:18:01.013 --> 00:18:02.714
using the turtle format,

364
00:18:02.714 --> 00:18:05.017
which you already saw in the previous lesson.

365
00:18:05.317 --> 00:18:07.719
And we specify the destination,

366
00:18:07.719 --> 00:18:11.023
so the file that we want to create for this knowledge graph.

367
00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:14.093
The resulting knowledge graph will be stored to disk

368
00:18:14.093 --> 00:18:16.061
and we can use it in the next lesson.

369
00:18:16.562 --> 00:18:18.864
In this lesson, you constructed a knowledge graph

370
00:18:19.164 --> 00:18:23.068
for the API data. And you saw how it is disconnected

371
00:18:23.368 --> 00:18:25.170
and the APIs are not connected.

372
00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:28.073
In the next lesson, you will learn how to integrate

373
00:18:28.474 --> 00:18:31.276
business process data in order to connect these APIs

374
00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:35.047
services according to their usage in business processes.
